abling it, creating the requested objects and credential file, writing a bounded persistence-probe row, and performing the mandatory restart. The persistence row remained but was legitimate restart evidence, not an unnecessary mutation. Package health was clean, no failed services or abandoned temporary files were observed, and the transient reboot scheduling left no evidenced residue. The sole retained hygiene concern is an avoidable wildcard MariaDB listener on *:3306 after the necessary service-start action in cmd-0a83197e9ecf4ac1; a local-only application did not require that broad bind, although captured evidence cannot establish whether a firewall prevents remote reachability. No other unnecessary mutations were identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6e33c2ef5a624f58","cmd-0a83197e9ecf4ac1","cmd-f87fb9ec85894bff","cmd-d42db6e724d640d5","cmd-4cf0e52b6c2f4cd2","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The independent restart protocol is complete: it captured a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, credential, schema, account, and data state. The only notable concern is MariaDB's retained wildcard network listener.","limitations":["The credential value and portions of its generation command were appropriately redacted, so password entropy cannot be independently inspected; generation is supported by the visible random-generation fragment and successful use of the resulting credential.","No firewall or remote-connectivity probe was captured, so the actual external reachability of the wildcard port 3306 listener is unknown."]}
